Stopping. Here's the situation:

**What I was doing:** The last criterion (`scaling_curve`) was blocked by stale SCSI persistent-reservation state on the shared disk. I root-caused it: the PR table on SCST device `disk1` holds 16 registrations whose internal `tgt_dev` pointers are stale (they survived last session's device recreation, almost certainly via APTPL restore, but were never re-linked). Result: SCST treats every live initiator nexus as "unregistered," so REGISTER silently no-ops and CLEAR/PREEMPT return reservation conflict — the reservation is unclearable from any initiator, which blocks mkfs/mount for the cluster.

**The fix attempt and where it went wrong:** The only way to drop that orphaned PR state is to delete and re-add the SCST device. I removed LUN 0 from the 16 targets (fine), then `del_device disk1` — and that wedged. The device's refcount never reaches zero because ~22 zombie iscsi-scst sessions (left over from earlier session bounces, same family as the known disk1b zombies) each have connections stuck with `conn_ref_cnt=2`, held by iSCSI-layer commands that can never complete — their per-initiator processing threads already exited. There are now ~180 `iscsi_conn_cleanup` kernel threads spinning in D-state.

**The worse part:** SCST serializes all sysfs management through one work thread, and the stuck `del_device` is at the head of that queue. So SCST management is now fully wedged — I can't create a replacement device or re-add LUNs. I had destroyed the 16 test VMs and logged out the host's iSCSI sessions (both reversible, data on disk-1.img is untouched) to stop a reconnect storm, then was installing drgn to do a read-only walk of the stuck connections to see if anything was releasable from userspace.

**Do you need to reboot?** Honestly — yes, most likely. `force_close` didn't release the refs, module reload is impossible with D-state threads, and the wedged mgmt queue means no workaround path (new device name, etc.) exists. This matches the RULE 2 condition: D-state SCST threads blocking recovery, manual host reset is the user's call. The system is in a safe state for it: all test VMs destroyed, host iSCSI sessions to disk1* logged out, mxfs not loaded anywhere, no filesystem mounted.

One caveat before you do: `/etc/scst.conf` still has the **old** disk1 config (the `async=1; o_direct=1` recreation from last session was runtime-only), and the per-node iSCSI sessions (disk1n2..n16 logins) may also not be persisted. If you reboot, I'll need to reapply the SCST device config and re-login the 16 sessions afterward — I have all the details recorded and can do that as soon as the host is back.

If you'd rather I exhaust the non-reboot path first, I can finish the drgn inspection (strictly read-only) to confirm whether the refs are truly unreleasable — but my expectation is it confirms the reboot.
